{"title":"2001 IMO Shortlist Problems/A5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/a/a2a25c2f9720d16d7ab307b6766d3539fad0a05b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"103\" height=\"13\" /> such that\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/e/2fef26a812b39c507d181bfee26d42c63a7e2fbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {99}{100} = \\frac {a_0}{a_1} + \\frac {a_1}{a_2} + \\cdots + \\frac {a_{n - 1}}{a_n},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"229\" height=\"40\" /></center>\n<p>where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/4/ea47b324908b7a67b59b71b4cb31334dfb4fe943.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_0 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/4/994ac72fe981f804809f12d8446e3b6275d27047.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{k + 1} - 1)a_{k - 1} \\geq a_k^2(a_k - 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"218\" height=\"19\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/5/5b537838ea7665cff69d6c9a92c9634f57894f7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 1,2,\\ldots,n - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"142\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We claim that there is only one such sequence: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/3/4b3878da23483a7d0a0978e9540379305df5ea82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1=2, a_2=5, a_3=56, a_4=56\\times 1400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"301\" height=\"16\" />. This works because\n\t\t\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/3/123bdc05a8f257935827cf657b7d80228884d4ac.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{56}+\\frac{56}{56\\times 1400}\\]\" width=\"192\" height=\"38\" />\n\t\t\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/e/f2ed1cecd2de333fd5538ce71663e9da451413b2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[=\\frac{700}{1400}+\\frac{560}{1400}+\\frac{125}{1400}+\\frac{1}{1400}=\\frac{1386}{1400}=\\frac{99}{100}.\\]\" width=\"370\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>\t\t\tIt is also easy to check that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/6/5c67d73b7d5efafe8bf3fd125dd32e9aedd6042c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{k+1}-1)a_{k-1} \\geq a_k^2(a_k - 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"218\" height=\"19\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/b/94b30ab29c97f2dcbe5a22231861d384b73653da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=1,2,3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>\t\t\tNow we prove such a sequence is unique. We first claim that a sequence of positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/9/ba9b97d1078af1a01094f48065b267d4574fddc7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_0, a_1,\\dots, a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"11\" /> satisfying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/6/5c67d73b7d5efafe8bf3fd125dd32e9aedd6042c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{k+1}-1)a_{k-1} \\geq a_k^2(a_k - 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"218\" height=\"19\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ce78f5225a2b104b7be75509dd95101abcddd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 1,2,\\ldots,n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"142\" height=\"16\" /> has the property that \n\t\t\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/2/3226f78f4d725a3cfb353ac3f52c6c0ca70abcf0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\frac{a_k}{a_{k+1}}&lt;\\frac{a_0}{a_1-1}.\\]\" width=\"147\" height=\"50\" />\n\t\t\tWe use induction on the length of the sequence.\n\t\t\tThe base case is trivial (the condition guarantees that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/a/8da9cfa8d9cd02104386314a76c5a7a06e939076.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" />). For the inductive step, notice that by the inductive hypothesis,\n\t\t\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/7/8577d21b667ca1e8c4b11aad89ea8f11558a1953.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\frac{a_k}{a_{k+1}}&lt;\\frac{a_1}{a_2-1},\\]\" width=\"147\" height=\"50\" />\n\t\t\tso it suffices to show\n\t\t\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/c/aec9224b4937da72e27f0f678bdbb4a3fc9ee352.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{a_0}{a_1}+\\frac{a_1}{a_2-1}\\le \\frac{a_0}{a_1-1},\\]\" width=\"174\" height=\"35\" />\n\t\t\tbut this is equivalent to \n\t\t\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/8/a88ea30a85d5b7f318da3c5b5011165840803dd4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_0(a_1-1)(a_2-1)+a_1^2(a_1-1)\\le a_0a_1(a_2-1)\\]\" width=\"362\" height=\"20\" />\n\t\t\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/f/95f96c55acc3221ca58c905211ff409be9389d99.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1^2(a_1-1)\\le a_0(a_2-1),\\]\" width=\"187\" height=\"19\" />\n\t\t\twhich we know to be true.\n</p><p>\t\t\tNow suppose there were two sequences <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/0/86001519bc528601e5b3ca97951345c8e2c2bd8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1,\\dots, a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/6/18638aeff88e0d68384bddf7387f835266670dc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&#39;_1,\\dots, a&#39;_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" /> that satisfied the conditions in the problem. Clearly one cannot be a subsequence of the other, or else the longer one will obviously have a larger sum. So there exists a smallest integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/1/111f083138e57511332223779c11c728e6f69a6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_c\\neq a&#39;_c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> (so for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/0/2401e7b710db7b722aba440b6376ff71d38a27a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&lt;c$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/b/00b91efe4b0d41fa865318ab0dff016b495691be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_k=a&#39;_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" />). Without loss of generality, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/d/b0d20ab6d8c3c65f2a25c44c50a1a6e3e1d4183f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_c&lt;a&#39;_c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>\t\t\tBy our previous claim, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/e/6ae832723968adc1af16344817ece1184a754538.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{k=c-1}^{n-1}\\frac{a&#39;_k}{a&#39;_{k+1}}&lt;\\frac{a&#39;_{c-1}}{a&#39;_c-1}=\\frac{a_{c-1}}{a&#39;_c-1}\\le\\frac{a_{c-1}}{a_c},\\]\" width=\"299\" height=\"50\" />\n\t\t\tand as a corollary, since the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/e/bbe4f88c83a69969cc48bb113da7a868e8b6ab1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c-2$\" width=\"38\" height=\"12\" /> terms are equal,\n\t\t\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/7/ab784924c5d5624cacd13aaf394072f8f0da3578.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\frac{a&#39;_k}{a&#39;_{k+1}}&lt;\\sum_{k=1}^{c-1}\\frac{a_k}{a_{k+1}}\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{m-1}\\frac{a_k}{a_{k+1}},\\]\" width=\"257\" height=\"51\" />\n\t\t\twhich contradicts the fact that they are equal. So there is a unique sequence satisfying the problem conditions.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Shortlist_Problems\" title=\"2001 IMO Shortlist Problems\">2001 IMO Shortlist Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?t=17452\">Discussion on AOPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017235311\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.016 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.024 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 153/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5850/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:7180:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017235311 and revision id 108503. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}